---
source: tests/release_workflow.rs
expression: rendered
---
echo "::notice title=Release automation is not configured::release-please did not run; the release environment holds no credentials. See the job log for the two a maintainer has to add."
cat <<'NOTICE'
The release environment of this repository holds no credentials, so the
release-please job did nothing. Nothing is broken and nothing is pending.

To turn release automation on, a maintainer with admin rights does three things:

  1. Install the release-please GitHub App on P4suta/ginary, granting it
     contents: write, pull-requests: write and issues: write.
  2. Add RELEASE_PLEASE_APP_CLIENT_ID, set to the App's client id, under
     Settings -> Environments -> release -> Environment variables.
  3. Add RELEASE_PLEASE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY, set to the App's private key, under
     Settings -> Environments -> release -> Environment secrets.

Both belong to the release environment rather than to repository scope. The
environment admits only the main branch and the v* tags, so neither value is
reachable from a pull request, from a fork, or from any other branch; a
repository-scope secret is readable by every workflow run there is.

The default GITHUB_TOKEN is deliberately not an alternative: this repository
sets can_approve_pull_request_reviews to false, so GitHub Actions may not
create pull requests, and that hardening stays.
NOTICE
